# modules/network/checks-reachability.rules - the NET-06 script-check
# registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5; data/scoursh-network-scan-design/
# report.md §3.1/§5.1, the NET-06 row in its §7 staged plan). Owned by
# modules/network/reachability.sh; ids are both `NET-PORT-*`.
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING PHASE SCRIPT, per rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's
# `checks-<name>.rules` row (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29) - modules/network/
# already carries no shared `checks.rules` for its script checks to collide
# on, so this ticket seeds directly under its own per-owner name rather than
# ever creating one, mirroring how modules/dast/passive/checks-cors.rules
# (DAST-08, landed after tension 29's split) was seeded.
#
# TWO CHECK IDS FOR ONE PROBE, on purpose - report.md §5.1's own reasoning,
# applied here exactly as it is for every sibling module in this tree: the
# `net` fingerprint location profile (lib/findings.sh, target host port
# transport) carries no component naming the STATE a listener was found in,
# so an `open`-and-violating-posture listener and a `not-open` listener on a
# DIFFERENT port of the SAME target would only collide if they somehow
# shared one id - they do not, because ports differ, but the underlying
# reasoning is the same one every DAST checks-*.rules file in this tree
# states for itself: severity and remediation genuinely differ between "this
# should be closed and is open" (a real exposure) and "this was declared and
# did not answer" (an inventory-hygiene observation), so a verdict that
# varies with the outcome is a SECOND check id, never one id whose severity
# is raised at runtime (AGENTS.md's own DAST-11 "a severity that varies with
# context" rule, applied one module down).
#
# `coverage-scope: target` is NET's cell (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's own
# NET row: the config/scope.conf target id) - a run scoped to one target can
# never manufacture a `fixed` verdict for a target it never visited.
#
# The type tag is `safe-active`, matching modules/network/engine.sh's own
# phase-table floor (`reachability.sh:safe`, CHECKS_INTENSITIES' `safe`
# tier): one TCP connect per declared listener, zero bytes sent, but a real
# connection to a port the operator did not necessarily expect a scanner to
# knock on - report.md §5.1's own table, and this file's own reachability.sh
# header, both say why that is `safe-active` rather than `passive`.

id: NET-PORT-UNEXPECTED_LISTENER-01
title: Declared listener answers on a port expected to be closed
script: reachability.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-668
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: Close this listener, restrict it to a private network or a
  VPN/bastion path, or add a firewall/security-group rule that denies the
  source this scan ran from, so the port no longer answers a plain TCP
  connect from an untrusted network. If it is now intentionally open, update
  or remove the corresponding config/posture.conf expectation
  (`check: NET-PORT-UNEXPECTED_LISTENER-01`, `scope-key: <target>:<port>`,
  `expect: absent`) so this baseline reflects the current, reviewed
  configuration rather than continuing to report drift.
references: CWE-668
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A05_2021-Security_Misconfiguration/

id: NET-PORT-DECLARED_NOT_ANSWERING-01
title: Declared network listener did not answer
script: reachability.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-16
owasp: A05:2021
tags: safe-active
coverage-scope: target
remediation: No action is required for this finding by itself - it records
  that a listener config/scope.conf declares for this target (base-url or an
  extra-host entry) did not accept a TCP connection during this run. If the
  listener is decommissioned, remove its extra-host entry from
  config/scope.conf so future runs do not keep probing a port nothing is
  meant to be running on; if it should be running, verify the service and
  any network path (firewall, security group, load balancer health check)
  between the scanner and the host.
references: CWE-16
